IMPORT prelude

-- wra1-three-valued.l4: technical helpers, not provisions of the Act.
-- Kleene three-valued AND, OR and NOT over MAYBE BOOLEAN, so that every function of the wra1 modules can combine
-- answers without turning NOTHING ("not covered") into FALSE (the rule of 2026-10-29: a caller must never do that).
--   JUST TRUE  = the text (or a supplied regulation) says yes
--   JUST FALSE = the text says no in its own words (or a supplied regulation does)
--   NOTHING    = not covered
-- Each helper is named under the provision where the combination is first needed, as PLAN.md section 2 requires.

§ `Three-valued logic over MAYBE BOOLEAN`

-- s 3(1): "(a) the basic conditions, and (b) the financial conditions": AND of two possibly unknown answers.
-- FALSE if either is FALSE (even if the other is NOTHING); TRUE only if both are TRUE; otherwise NOTHING.
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN, b IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) both of these are true` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    -- FALSE-kind (Kleene, not a rule of the Act): one known no decides an AND
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN b
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER b
        -- FALSE-kind (Kleene, not a rule of the Act): one known no decides an AND
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (Kleene, not a rule of the Act): no known no, and one answer is not known
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 19(2): "(a) ... (b) ... (c) ... or (d)": OR of two possibly unknown answers.
-- TRUE if either is TRUE; FALSE only if both are FALSE; otherwise NOTHING.
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN, b IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 19(2) either of these is true` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN b
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER b
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (Kleene, not a rule of the Act): no known yes, and one answer is not known
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 4(1)(d): "is not receiving education": NOT of a possibly unknown answer.
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(d) the opposite of this` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    -- FALSE-kind (Kleene, not a rule of the Act): the opposite of a known yes
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (Kleene, not a rule of the Act): the opposite of what is not known is not known
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
